METHOD
For every half-level 1<=K<=6, the audit exhausts every nonzero
character-positive profile p whose K+1 coefficients lie in {0,1,2,3}.
It constructs the exact finite fusion square d=p*p, tests whether d is
log-concave with interval support, and evaluates every all-radius
anchored current.  It separately counts the no-affine-wall coordinates
R+S<=K and the profiles satisfying every simple-current boundary
inequality

  d_0 d_(K-R)-d_R d_K >= 0.

For each such boundary-admissible profile it then checks every current

  d_0 (N_R d)_S-d_R d_S,             1<=R,S<=K.

It also tests the two structured shape classes singled out by the
factor-word reduction: log-concave p at the simple-current boundary,
and profiles for which both p and p*p are log-concave at every current.
For the former class it also checks every adjacent reflected-ratio
margin, locating the exact negative value -120 which shows that only
the cumulative boundary order survives.  It then checks the increments
of that cumulative order and locates the exact positive recrossing 18,
so unimodality of the boundary current is false as well.

All arithmetic is integer arithmetic in boost::multiprecision::cpp_int.
The displayed negative log-concave case is therefore an exact
counterexample to the finite abstraction "character-positive square
plus log-concavity implies the anchored cone."  The exhaustive counts
are bounded diagnostics.  In particular, the absence of a negative
log-concave no-wall current and of a negative boundary-admissible
current is discovery evidence, not a proof of either unbounded claim.
The single displayed negative integer identity is the counterexample.

COMPILER FLAGS
